<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vietnam War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> War was fought from the mid-1950s to 1975 between communist North Vietnam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> on one side and South Vietnam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on the other, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and others sending forces. American combat troops fought from 1965 until their withdrawal in 1973, and the war ended with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 and Vietnam's reunification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vietnam-war.jpg" alt="Vietnam War" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Air Force (Operation Holly 1970 (Folder 13 of 15), sheet 182) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Army_UH-1H_Hueys_insert_ARVN_troops_at_Kh%C3%A2m_%C4%90%E1%BB%A9c,_Vietnam,_12_July_1970_(79431435).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Zealand forces fought in the Malayan Emergency and in the Indonesian–Malaysian confrontation of the 1960s, and from 1964 engineers, artillery and later infantry served in the <b>Vietnam War</b>, a commitment that provoked a large protest movement; the last troops were withdrawn by the end of 1972.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Socialist Republic of Vietnam was born from the victory of the north in the <b>Vietnam War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>King also spoke out against the <b>Vietnam War</b> in 1967 and planned a Poor People’s Campaign, while the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover, which had wiretapped him since 1963, sought to discredit him.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1968 the <b>Vietnam War</b> had given rise to the New Left, critical of both the Soviet Union and social democracy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Judy Collins recorded it in 1970 in opposition to the <b>Vietnam War</b>, and it reached number 15 on the Billboard Hot 100; an instrumental by the Royal Scots Dragoon Guards spent five weeks at number one in the UK in 1972.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>Because of these images of peace, the spiritual has also been sung as an anti-war protest song, especially during the <b>Vietnam War</b>, and it appears in collections of socialist and labour songs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/down-by-the-riverside">Down by the Riverside</a></li></ul></section>
